<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Laskarid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1204–1261</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Laskaris dynasty, Laskarids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Laskarid dynasty ruled the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, the main Byzantine successor state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, from 1204 to 1261. Its emperors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-i-laskaris">Theodore I Laskaris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-doukas-vatatzes">John III Doukas Vatatzes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-ii-laskaris">Theodore II Laskaris</a> and the child John IV, rebuilt a Byzantine state and recovered much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and Macedonia. Their court was often at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nymphaion">Nymphaion</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a> usurped the throne and retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1261.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/laskarid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Laskarid dynasty" width="320" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dumbarton Oaks · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aspron_trachy_of_Theodore_I_Laskaris.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
